//! #7602: a `?//` bind in a builtin's argument inside `map(f)`, `with_entries(f)` or `walk(f)` over
//! a value the program built retries the next alternative when the builtin refuses the first one's
//! value, as in jq: `["x"] | map(startswith([1, "x"] as [$a] ?// [$b, $a] | $a))` is `[true]`, where
//! it raised `startswith() requires string inputs`. jq defines the three as `[.[] | f]`,
//! `to_entries | map(f) | from_entries` and `walk`'s recursive `def`, so `f` runs as a stage of a
//! pipe; the builtins' own element walks ran it where a failure could not retry the bind, so such
//! an `f` is now spelled out as jq's definition.
//!
//! Every expectation was captured from jq 1.7.1 (`/usr/bin/jq`) over a generated matrix: ten
//! builtins that refuse the first alternative's value, eight shapes around the call (bare, `if`,
//! `select`, an object, `| not`, an array, `try`, a comma), and eight hosts (`map` over one and two
//! built elements, `to_entries | map`, `with_entries`, `map(map(f))`, `walk`, and two consumers of
//! the result). jq 1.8.2 agrees on every row kept (its `ltrimstr` and error quoting differ on a
//! few others, which are not about the retry and are left out).

use anyhow::Result;

